        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The STS-79 mission, which launched on September 16, 1996, was the fourth docking of the U.S. Space Shuttle with Russia's Mir Space Station. The Shuttle crew included Commander William F. Readdy, Pilot Terrence W. Wilcutt, Mission Specialists Jay Apt, Carol E. Walz, Thomas D. Akers, and John Blaha, the NASA 3 crewmember who replaced the Shannon Lucid aboard Mir. Dr. Lucid took John Blaha's place on the Shuttle for the return trip to Earth. The exchange of these crewmembers on Mir ushered in the NASA 3 mission and marked the end of a successful NASA 2 mission. In addition, STS-79 was the first Shuttle mission to carry a Spacehab double module. This module, located in the payload bay, housed experiments conducted by the crew before, during and after Atlantis docked with Mir and resupply items, such as food, clothing, experiment supplies and spare equipment, to be transferred to Mir.<P>During the five days of joint operations, astronauts and cosmonauts transferred science samples and data collected by the Mir 21/NASA 2 crew from Mir to the Shuttle. After returning to Earth, the information was analyzed by researchers on the ground. Crewmembers also transferred hardware and supplies to Mir in support of the health and well being of the Mir 22/NASA 3 crew, as well as supporting future investigations on the station. Many of the resupply science items were directly utilized to support scientific investigations during the ensuing long-duration mission, NASA 3.<P>Several experiments were performed during STS-79 in the fields of advanced technology, Earth sciences, fundamental biology, human life sciences, microgravity, and space sciences.  Data from these experiments will supply insight for the planning and development of the International Space Station, Earth-based sciences of human and biological processes, and the advancement of commercial technology. <P>Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) were also performed on this mission.  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation that is performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>Additional payloads on board STS-79 were the IMAX camera, used to document activities on the shuttle and Mir, and the Shuttle Amateur Radio Experiment (SAREX-II), in which ham radio operators and students attempted to make radio contact with the orbiting Shuttle.<P>  The variety of science coupled with the ability to conduct investigations over long periods in microgravity, made the Shuttle-Mir missions unique and extremely valuable. Nearly all Shuttle-Mir investigations were joint efforts between the U.S. and Russia, with at least one researcher involved from each country. The knowledge gained will assist researchers in developing future space stations and procedures for operating on board the facility, and will advance the knowledge base of an array of science areas to the benefit of all people on Earth.<END>
        </div>

